<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);MSB3277</NoWarn>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <!--
      Solution-wide warnings-as-errors — every csc/analyzer warning fails the
      build. Combined with EnforceCodeStyleInBuild=true above this pulls the
      IDExxxx style diagnostics into the same gate. If a specific rule ever
      needs an exception, add its ID to NoWarn ABOVE this line rather than
      turning the flag off. Enabled 2026-08-24 after the 15-warning cleanup.
    -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    NuGet vulnerability auditing. Every one of these is already the net10.0
    SDK default today; they are pinned here so the gate cannot silently move
    when the SDK bumps, and so the severity choice below is reviewable.

    NuGetAuditMode=all audits transitive dependencies, not just direct ones -
    which is where advisories almost always land for us.

    NuGetAuditLevel=moderate is a DELIBERATE step up from the SDK's `low`.
    Audit findings surface as NU1901-NU1904 warnings, and TreatWarningsAsErrors
    above promotes them to build errors, on a pipeline that is a *blocking*
    branch policy on main. At `low`, a newly published low-severity advisory
    against any transitive package halts every merge overnight with no code
    change on our side. `moderate` keeps the hard gate for anything that
    actually matters without letting the noise floor stop work. Revisit when
    there is a prod environment to protect.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>moderate</NuGetAuditLevel>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Static security analysis. .NET's own analyzers are already enabled
    (EnableNETAnalyzers defaults true on net10.0), but AnalysisMode=Default
    leaves most of the CA security category switched OFF - including the
    CA3001-CA3012 injection rules and most of the CA5xxx crypto rules.

    AnalysisModeSecurity=All turns on that category and ONLY that category, so
    we get the security rules without dragging in every design, naming and
    performance rule at the same time. Findings become build errors via
    TreatWarningsAsErrors above, same as any other diagnostic.

    Suppressions are per-site with a written justification (see CA2100 and
    CA5394 sites), never a blanket NoWarn here. A NoWarn on CA5394 would also
    cover the day someone reaches for Random to generate a token, which is the
    exact bug the rule exists to catch.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AnalysisModeSecurity>All</AnalysisModeSecurity>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Assembly + root namespace convention: every project ships as {{SOLUTION}}.{ProjectName}
    (and matching root namespace). Skipped when the project name already starts with
    "{{SOLUTION}}." to prevent double-prefixing. Per-project overrides win when set in
    the csproj itself.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="!$(MSBuildProjectName.StartsWith('{{SOLUTION}}.'))">
    <AssemblyName>{{SOLUTION}}.$(MSBuildProjectName)</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>{{SOLUTION}}.$(MSBuildProjectName.Replace(' ', '_'))</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
